#!/usr/bin/env perl
# PODNAME: raider
# ABSTRACT: Autonomous CLI agent with filesystem and bash access

use strict;
use warnings;

our $VERSION = '0.503';

use Langertha::Raider::CLI::Main;

binmode STDOUT, ':encoding(UTF-8)';
binmode STDIN,  ':encoding(UTF-8)';

exit Langertha::Raider::CLI::Main->new->run(@ARGV);

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    raider "Find all TODO comments in lib/ and summarize them"
    raider -e openai -m gpt-4o "Run the test suite and fix any trivial failures"
    raider -i -r ~/dev/myproject
    raider --perl --pack testing-fu "Run prove -l t and explain failures"
    raider --json "Summarize README.md" > result.json
    raider config explain -m gpt-4o
    raider hall start --acp-port 38421
    raider acp prompt 127.0.0.1:38421 --raider bjorn "Summarize status"

=head1 DESCRIPTION

F<raider> is the CLI front-end for L<Langertha::Raider::CLI>. It wires an LLM engine
(via L<Langertha>) to a filesystem tool server and L<MCP::Run::Bash>,
then runs the L<Langertha::Raider> multi-turn agent loop on your prompt.
Optional profile, pack, and Perl-native tool flags let the same CLI load
project agent instructions, persona/power bundles, and C<perl_eval> /
C<perl_check> / C<perl_cpanm> tools.

Interactive mode (C<-i>) opens a REPL with conversation history retained
across turns. With L<Term::ReadLine::Gnu> installed, line editing and
persistent input history (F<~/.raider_history>) are enabled automatically —
including across Ctrl-C interrupts. Slash commands available in the REPL:
C</help>, C</clear>, C</metrics>, C</stats>, C</reload>, C</prompt>,
C</skill>, C</skill-claude>, C</config>, C</model>, C</packs>, C</pack>,
C</quit>.

Subcommands are dispatched before normal raider startup: C<raider hall ...>
manages a Raider Hall daemon, and C<raider acp ...> runs the bundled ACP
client. C<raider config explain [options]> takes the normal options and
prints every effective setting with its source -- a command-line flag, a
F<.raider.yml> layer, an environment variable or the built-in default --
without writing F<.raider.yml> or contacting a model; C</config> prints the
same inside the REPL. The options may also come first
(C<raider -e openai config explain>); there only the exact words
C<config explain> are the subcommand, any other prompt starting with
C<config> is sent as a prompt.

With C<--json>, C<--msgpack> or C<--yaml>, F<raider> prints one document
describing the run instead of the human-oriented output, which is useful
for scripting; see L</MACHINE OUTPUT>.

When standard input is not a terminal, the REPL (C<-i>) reads its lines
from it and ends at its end.

=head1 MACHINE OUTPUT

C<--json>, C<--msgpack> and C<--yaml> print one document for the run, once,
when it has ended. Nothing else goes to standard output: the live trace is
off unless C<--trace> asks for it, and then goes to standard error like
every diagnostic. The flags exclude each other; giving two is a usage
error. A usage or configuration error (exit status C<2> or C<3>) prints no
document, only its message on standard error.

The three flags write the same model in three encodings:

=over

=item C<--json> -- a pretty-printed JSON object with sorted keys, UTF-8.

=item C<--msgpack> -- one MessagePack map; text is written as UTF-8 C<str>,
never C<bin>. Standard output is binary.

=item C<--yaml> -- one YAML document, starting with C<--->, UTF-8.

=back

=head2 The document

A run that completed:

    {
       "elapsed" : 3,
       "metrics" : { "iterations" : 2, "raids" : 1, "time_ms" : 2710.4, "tool_calls" : 1 },
       "response" : "The README describes ...",
       "status" : "completed",
       "version" : 1
    }

A run that failed:

    { "elapsed" : 0, "error" : "...", "status" : "failed", "version" : 1 }

=over

=item C<version> -- integer, the format version (see below).

=item C<status> -- how the run ended: C<completed> or C<failed>. These are
the run states of the redesign (ADR 0009) that end a run and that the CLI
can produce today; C<cancelled>, C<refused> and C<interrupted> are reserved
for when it can.

=item C<response> -- the agent's final answer (C<completed> only).

=item C<metrics> -- the raider's cumulative metrics: C<raids>,
C<iterations>, C<tool_calls>, C<time_ms> (C<completed> only).

=item C<error> -- the error message (C<failed> only).

=item C<elapsed> -- seconds the run took.

=back

=head2 Versions

C<--json=N> (C<--msgpack=N>, C<--yaml=N>) asks for format version C<N>; the
version belongs to the model, so it means the same in every encoding. The
only version is C<1>, which is also what the plain flags write; any other
C<N> is a usage error. Only the C<=N> form carries a version -- in
C<raider --json 2 ...> the C<2> is part of the prompt.

Within a version, fields are only ever added, never renamed, removed or
changed in meaning, so a consumer must ignore fields it does not know. A
breaking change is a new version, chosen with C<--json=2>; the plain flag
stays on the old version for at least one release after the new one ships.

=head1 EXIT STATUS

=over

=item C<0> -- success, including C<--help>, the exports, C<config explain>
and leaving the REPL.

=item C<1> -- the run failed: the engine, a tool or the network raised an
error. With a machine output flag, the document says C<failed>.

=item C<2> -- usage error: unknown option, a C<-o> that is not
C<KEY=VALUE>, an unknown C<config> subcommand, no prompt, more than one
machine output flag, or an unknown format version.

=item C<3> -- configuration error: F<.raider.yml> cannot be read, or the
engine is unknown.

=back

The C<hall> and C<acp> subcommands keep their own exit statuses.

API keys come from standard environment variables
(C<ANTHROPIC_API_KEY>, C<OPENAI_API_KEY>, etc.).
Claude Code subscription/OAuth credentials are not consumed by this CLI;
Anthropic usage goes through the API key path.

=seealso

=over

=item * L<Langertha::Raider::CLI>

=item * L<Langertha::Raider>

=back

=cut
